@meta
id: 12-11
title: Search is a motion
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-motion
par: 34
minutes: 6
vscode: click at the start, shift-click at the end, Delete
open: order.resolver.ts

@brief
Somebody debugged the `orders` query in production and left the evidence
behind. Five lines of timing and logging sit between the top of the
method and the `return`. All five go.

Everything up to now in this chapter has treated search as a way to get
somewhere. This is the lesson where that changes. Anywhere vim accepts a
motion — and `d`, `c`, `y`, `>` all do — it accepts a search. You are not
limited to `w`, `}` and `G`; you can say "to the next place the word
`return` appears" and mean it as an edit.

That is the whole difference between a find box and a search command.
Ctrl+F finds. This edits.

@keys
| `d/text` `Enter` | delete from the cursor up to the next match of `text` |
| `c/text` `Enter` | change that same range |
| `y/text` `Enter` | yank it |
| `/text` `Enter` | plain search, as before, to get to the start |

@hints
Do not count the lines and do not delete them one at a time. Get the
cursor to the first junk line and then describe where the deletion
should stop.
--
An operator takes a motion, and a search is a motion. `d` followed by
`/something` `Enter` deletes everything between here and that match.
--
`/const started` `Enter` puts you at the start of the junk. `d/return`
`Enter` then deletes from there up to — but not including — the `return`,
which is exactly the five lines. The indentation works out because both
lines are indented the same way.

@solution
/const started<Enter>  d/return<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes to delete a five-line block without counting
anything, and it would be the same twenty-eight if the block were fifty
lines.

The mental model to take away: `d` needs to know where to stop, and a
search is a perfectly good way of saying where. `d}` stops at the end of
the paragraph, `dG` stops at the end of the file, `d/return` stops at the
next `return`. Same grammar, one more noun.

Two details that matter in practice. A `/` search motion is *exclusive* —
the match itself is not included, the deletion stops just before the `r`
of `return`. That is usually what you want, because the thing you
searched for is usually the landmark you want to keep. When it is not,
the next lessons show you how to include it.

And because the deletion started at the `c` of `const`, mid-line, the
indentation in front of it survived and the `return` slid up into it.
Starting an operator at the first non-blank character and ending it at
the first non-blank character of another line is a small habit that keeps
indentation intact for free.

@start order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { RefundProvider } from '../refund/refund.provider';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(RefundProvider) protected refundProvider: RefundProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
  ) {}

  public async orders(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const started = Date.now();
    console.log('[orders] called for', args.customerId);
    console.log('[orders] pid', process.pid);
    console.table({ customerId: args.customerId, at: new Date().toISOString() });
    console.log('[orders] elapsed ms', Date.now() - started);
    return this.orderProvider.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.customerId });
  }

  public async order(args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne({ id: args.id });
  }

  public async createOrder(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    return this.orderProvider.create(args);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel({ id: args.id });
  }

  public async refundOrder(args: { id: string; amount: number }) {
    return this.refundProvider.partial({ id: args.id, amount: args.amount });
  }

  public async orderCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);
  }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { RefundProvider } from '../refund/refund.provider';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(RefundProvider) protected refundProvider: RefundProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
  ) {}

  public async orders(args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.customerId });
  }

  public async order(args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne({ id: args.id });
  }

  public async createOrder(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    return this.orderProvider.create(args);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel({ id: args.id });
  }

  public async refundOrder(args: { id: string; amount: number }) {
    return this.refundProvider.partial({ id: args.id, amount: args.amount });
  }

  public async orderCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);
  }
}
